============================================================
WHAT MAKES AN EDGE UNCOVERED?
============================================================

  bits=8, uncovered edge 0→4:
    Common out-neighbors of 0,4: []
    Common in-neighbors of 0,4: []
    Intermediates (i→k→j): []
    out-deg(0)=1, out-deg(4)=3

  bits=14, uncovered edge 1→0:
    Common out-neighbors of 1,0: []
    Common in-neighbors of 1,0: []
    Intermediates (i→k→j): []
    out-deg(1)=1, out-deg(0)=3

  bits=24, uncovered edge 0→4:
    Common out-neighbors of 0,4: []
    Common in-neighbors of 0,4: []
    Intermediates (i→k→j): []
    out-deg(0)=1, out-deg(4)=3

  Edge i→j is uncovered iff:
    - No k with (i→j, j→k, i→k) [common out-neighbor]
    - No k with (k→i, i→j, k→j) [common in-neighbor that also beats j]
    - No k with (i→k, k→j, i→j) [intermediate on transitive path]
    = No vertex k forming a transitive triple with edge i→j
    = For all k: either k→i or j→k (k 'reverses' the path through i→j)

  INSIGHT: edge i→j is uncovered iff every other vertex k satisfies:
    k→i OR j→k (or both)
  i.e., every k either beats i or loses to j.
  In other words: N_in(i) ∪ N_out(j) ⊇ V\{i,j}
  Equivalently: N_out(i)\{j} ⊆ N_out(j)\{i}
  The out-neighborhood of i (minus j) is CONTAINED in that of j!

  Verifying containment characterization at n=5...
    Correct: 6080, Wrong: 4160

  Verifying containment characterization at n=6...
    Correct: 337920, Wrong: 153600

============================================================
SUMMARY OF FINDINGS
============================================================

  1. Edge i→j is NOT covered by any transitive triple
     iff N_out(i)\{j} ⊆ N_out(j)\{i}
     (j dominates all of i's other out-neighbors)

  2. β₁ > 0 ⟹ ∃ such an edge (full coverage implies β₁=0)

  3. The unbounding cycle PASSES THROUGH the uncovered edge(s)

  4. GRAPH-THEORETIC MEANING: An uncovered edge i→j means
     j is "almost dominating" i — j beats everyone i beats.
     This creates a local ordering i < j that can't be
     consistently extended to a global acyclic structure
     via transitive triples.

